atic and prediction markets price a Gray win near 95%", "No general-election polling exists, and the district's underlying Democratic lean is only about two points"], "rationale": "Gray's incumbency, his clear money advantage, and redistricting that added Democratic-leaning Stockton outweigh the seat's narrow partisan lean. Rating outlets mostly call the race Likely or Lean Democratic, markets price about 95% for Gray, and the fundamentals-based estimate of a high-single-digit to low-double-digit margin fits all three.", "watch": "Any late polling showing Lincoln within single digits, or a national environment shifting toward Republicans, would narrow this forecast.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Adam Gray (D) is running."]}, "overview": "Adam Gray, a first-term Democrat, seeks reelection in a Central Valley district that redistricting reshaped to include Stockton. The seat carries only a slight Democratic lean, and Gray won it narrowly in 2024, but he enters the general with clear advantages: he has raised roughly twice as much as Republican Kevin Lincoln, a former mayor, and holds a large cash-on-hand edge. Outside spending has been minimal on both sides.\n\nNo general-election polls are available, so the picture rests on fundamentals and outside assessments. Rating outlets mostly place the race in the Likely or Lean Democratic column, and prediction markets price a Democratic win at about 95%. A midterm environment that appears favorable to Democrats, plus Speaker Mike Johnson's visit to stump for Lincoln, frames the contest. The forecast expects Gray to win by a comfortable but not overwhelming margin.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.9502, "margin": 10.95}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9343, "margin": 10.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.945, "margin": 11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.941, "margin": 10.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26"